  "body": "## What changed\n\nThis is slice 2 of #1445: the boring cycle (G86: feed in, spindle stop, rapid out). The `Bore` cycle kind was already on main. This slice pins it per post and closes two dwell gaps.\n\n- `assets/posts/{fanuc,haas,mazak,doosan-dnm,sinumerik}.cps`: a bore with a dwell is refused by name. Before this change, these posts wrote `P<ms>` on G86. The published Fanuc and Haas G86 formats have no P word, so the control dropped the dwell without a message. A bore with no dwell posts as before.\n- tormach and grblhal keep `G86 ... P<seconds>` (the LinuxCNC G86 takes P as a dwell in seconds).\n- The expanding posts (heidenhain, tinyg, carvera) refuse a bore by name on main (no spindle stop before the rapid retract). The tests pin that refusal and the app writes no NC file. Rejected intermediate program text can still contain fallback moves.\n- New door `OpParams::dwell_refusal` (`crates/app/src/operation/types/dwell.rs`): a drilling dwell is a time from 0 to 3600 s (`MAX_DWELL_S`). `CreateOperation` and `EditOperationParams` refuse other values by name. MCP `edit_operation` uses the same command, so it refuses them too. The project loader does not use this door, so an old project still opens.\n- Decision record: `docs/decisions/1445-boring-g86.md`.\n\nThe default posted output does not change. No existing NC or CL golden changed.\n\n## Sources\n\n- NIST IR 6556 (The NIST RS274NGC Interpreter v3), G86.\n- The LinuxCNC G-code reference (G86 with P in seconds).\n- The public Fanuc 30i operator manual (B-63944EN) and the Haas mill programming workbook, for the G86 address words.\n\nNo vendor post file or CAM output was read.\n\n## How tested\n\n- `crates/post/tests/bore_cycle.rs` (7 tests): the stream round trip, G86 and no other cycle on the canned posts, the by-name dwell refusal on the Fanuc family and Sinumerik, `P0.5` on tormach and grblhal, the by-name refusal of the expanding posts, the refusal on posts without drilling, and new goldens in `crates/post/tests/golden/bore/` (fanuc, haas, sinumerik, grblhal, tormach).\n- `crates/app/tests/bore_cycle.rs` (4 tests): undo and redo of a bore with a dwell and its generated chain, the by-name refusal of an insane dwell at command creation and edit, the MCP `edit_operation` door, and `refused_expanded_bores_write_no_nc_file` for all three expanding posts with and without a dwell.\n- `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main`: green (154 steps, 0 failed). App/post all-target Clippy passes with `-D warnings`. Each review fix was mutation-checked: RED when removed, GREEN when restored.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- No real control ran this output: Fanuc, Haas, Mazak, Doosan, Sinumerik, Heidenhain, grblHAL, PathPilot, TinyG and Carvera.\n- The grblHAL and Sinumerik ISO forms of G86 with P are not verified from a manual.\n- The P dwell on the Fanuc-family G83 is not checked (follow-up below).\n- The GUI was not launched. The batch tier was not run.\n\n## Follow-ups\n\n- #1819: the expanded bore on heidenhain, tinyg and carvera stops the spindle at the bottom.\n- #1820: check that the Fanuc-family posts do not drop a dwell on G83.\n- #1479 (fine boring G76), #1480 (G89), #1481 (ream on lathe posts) stay open from slice 1.\n\nChangelog: The Fanuc, Haas, Mazak, Doosan and Sinumerik posts refuse a bore (G86) with a dwell by name, because their G86 has no dwell word; a drilling dwell outside 0 to 3600 s is refused when the operation is made or edited (#1445)\n\nEffort: high (no escalation)\n\nRefs #1445\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n",
